question: Does a combined test that rejects when either Shapiro-Wilk or plain S2 rejects, each calibrated so the union has exact 5% size at n=400, beat both single tests? With 20000 paired draws and a new seed, report its power beside SW and S2 under lognormal sigma 0.1 and 0.2, skew-normal-scaled t df 10 at sqrt(beta1) near 0.3, and t df 5, with paired differences union minus each test and their paired SE.
learning: Combined skew and heavy tails split the winner at n=400: across three seeds at exact 5% size with 20000 paired draws, Shapiro-Wilk leads plain S2 beyond two paired SE on lognormal sigma 0.1 and 0.2, but S2 leads beyond two paired SE on skew-normal-scaled t df 10 at sqrt(beta1) near 0.3, where S2-only rejections are about twice SW-only. Lognormal sigma 0.3 is at the power ceiling for both, so neither test dominates mixed alternatives.
